### STEP 3 -- The DUT serves time: the exchange, field by field

```
tools/tb capture tb277 ntp1021 eth3 25 t33399-s3-on.pcap udp port 123       (background)
tools/tb tool tb277 ntp1021 ntpc 10.38.22.34 --bind 10.38.22.33 --count 3 --gap 2 --timeout 3
```

```
  #1 sent 2026-10-21 03:32:58.526595Z  reply 48 B from 10.38.22.34:123
      LI=0(no-warning) VN=4 mode=4(server) stratum=5 poll=4 precision=-25
      refid=127.127.1.1 (ascii '....')  root_delay=0.000000s root_dispersion=0.000000s
      reference=2026-10-21 03:31:59.885092Z
      origin   =2026-10-21 03:32:58.526531Z  origin_echo=yes
      receive  =2026-10-21 03:32:44.257702Z
      transmit =2026-10-21 03:32:44.257816Z
      offset=-14.269142 s  delay=0.000498 s   (server clock minus ours)
  ... #2 and #3 identical in every field except the timestamps

SUMMARY: sent=3 replies=3 origin_echo=3 no_reply=0
         reply stratum(s)=5 mode(s)=4
         offset min=-14.269191 max=-14.269128 mean=-14.269154 s
```

Graded field by field:

| field | measured | why it is right |
| --- | --- | --- |
| mode | **4 (server)** | the DUT answered as a server, not as a peer |
| LI | **0, no warning** | it is claiming to be synchronised, not LI=3 |
| stratum | **5** | exactly the `ntp master 5` value -- the configuration reached the packet |
| refid | **127.127.1.1** | the local-clock reference, and it matches `show ntp status`'s `Reference ID: 7F7F0101` byte for byte (0x7F7F0101 = 127.127.1.1) |
| origin | **echoes our transmit, `origin_echo=yes` on all 3** | the reply is an answer to *this* request |
| root delay / dispersion | **0 / 0** | consistent with a stand-alone master whose reference is itself |
| offset | **-14.269 s**, spread 63 microseconds over 3 samples | the DUT's clock relative to the box, now measured rather than eyeballed |

Independent witness, `ntpwire` on the capture:
```
  by mode:   client(3)=3  server(4)=3
  10.38.22.33 -> 10.38.22.34   3
  10.38.22.34 -> 10.38.22.33   3
  pairing:   requests=3 replies=3  paired=3 unpaired=0 unanswered=0
```
Three requests, three replies, three paired, nothing unpaired. The two instruments agree exactly.

**STEP 3 PASS.**

### STEP 4 -- The server answers on every IP interface, not just one

Second leg, a different host NIC, a different DUT SVI, same server:
```
tools/tb tool tb277 ntp1021 ntpc 10.38.22.66 --bind 10.38.22.65 --count 2 --gap 2 --timeout 3
  -> LI=0 mode=4 stratum=5 refid=127.127.1.1, offset -14.2692 s
  SUMMARY: sent=2 replies=2 origin_echo=2 no_reply=0
```
`ntp master` is a global service: it serves on **vlan48 `10.38.22.66`** exactly as on vlan10,
with the same stratum and refid. There is no per-interface enable, and none is offered in the
CLI surface.

**STEP 4 PASS.**

### STEP 5 -- `ntp restrict` DISCRIMINATES BY CLIENT, measured in the same minute

This is the control that must not match, run against a live server rather than a dead one --
the strongest form available, because the only thing that differs between the two legs is the
client's address.

```
awplus(config)#ntp restrict 10.38.22.33 serve deny
awplus#show running-config | include ntp
ntp master 5
ntp restrict 10.38.22.33 serve deny
```

| leg | client | result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **denied** | 10.38.22.33 (eth3) | `sent=3 replies=0 origin_echo=0 no_reply=3`, ntpc exit 2 |
| **allowed**, same server, ~20 s later | 10.38.22.65 (eth4) | `sent=2 replies=2 origin_echo=2 no_reply=0`, stratum 5 |

`ntpwire` on the denied leg's capture:
```
  by mode:   client(3)=3
  10.38.22.33 -> 10.38.22.34   3
  pairing:   requests=3 replies=0  paired=0 unpaired=0 unanswered=3
```
**Three requests left the box and the DUT answered none of them**, while it was answering the
other client throughout. The denial is **silent** -- no kiss-o'-death, no ICMP, nothing. That is
correct NTP server behaviour for a `deny` restriction and it is worth knowing, because a tester
reading only the client side cannot tell "denied" from "unreachable".

Removed, and the service came back on the same leg:
```
awplus(config)#no ntp restrict 10.38.22.33
awplus#show running-config | include ntp
ntp master 5
  -> ntpc 10.38.22.34 --bind 10.38.22.33: sent=2 replies=2 origin_echo=2, stratum 5
```
So the off/on/removed arc closes on the wire, not on a `show`.

**STEP 5 PASS.**

### STEP 6 -- The advertised stratum is the configured stratum

```
awplus(config)#ntp master 10          ! REPLACES `ntp master 5`; the config keeps ONE line
awplus#show running-config | include ntp
ntp master 10
awplus#show ntp status
Reference ID    : 7F7F0101 ()
Stratum         : 10
...
  -> ntpc: SUMMARY sent=2 replies=2 origin_echo=2; reply stratum(s)=10 mode(s)=4
```
The CLI value, the `show` value and the value in the served packet are the same number. Changing
it changes the wire.

**STEP 6 PASS.**

### STEP 7 -- 🔴 The default rate limiter, which is why some requests go unanswered

Noticed, not hunted for: a 4-packet run answered 3, a 10-packet run answered 9. On a bench that
otherwise pings 3/3 that is the kind of result a tester is tempted to call noise, so it was
measured instead.

| client send pattern | answered |
| --- | --- |
| 20 packets, 0.5 s apart | **11 / 20** |
| 5 packets, 8 s apart | 4 / 5 |
| 10 packets, 1 s apart | 9 / 10 |

The mechanism is in the DUT's own help text:
```
awplus(config)#ntp rate-limit ?
  burst     Maximum number of responses that can be sent in a burst, default: 8
  interval  Minimum number of seconds between responses in seconds, default: 8 seconds
  leak      Rate at which responses are allowed to exceed limits, default: 4 (allow every 4th request)
```
**Rate limiting is ON BY DEFAULT on this platform** -- burst 8, interval 8 s, leak 4 -- and
nothing in `show running-config` says so. The measured 11/20 is exactly a burst of 8 followed by
one in four leaking through.

Proved by changing it, which is the control that turns an observation into a mechanism:

| configuration | the identical 20-packet / 0.5 s burst |
| --- | --- |
| default (nothing configured) | **11 / 20** answered |
| `ntp rate-limit leak 2` | **16 / 20** answered |
| `ntp rate-limit burst 64` | **20 / 20** answered |

**STEP 7 PASS** -- and it is Finding 2, the single most useful thing in this case for whoever
tests NTP next.

### STEP 8 -- Persistence across a cold boot, measured ON THE WIRE

The limb three campaigns could not run. With `ntp master 10` in running-config:

```
awplus#write
Building configuration...
[OK]
```
startup-config diff against the campaign baseline -- the write added exactly:
```
> !
> ntp master 10
```
plus the updated `! Startup-config saved on Wed Oct 21 03:37:11 2026 +0000 ...` comment. Nothing
else was saved.

```
tools/tb ckreload tb277 ntp1021 u2 115200 240
16:37:42 >>> reload
reboot question: 'reboot system? (y/n):' -> y
16:38:43 login seen after 60 s
```
**60 s from `reload` to the login prompt** -- a third independent confirmation of group C's
measurement.

Then, **with nothing typed into the DUT at all** -- no login, no configuration, 13 s after the
login prompt appeared:
```
tools/tb capture tb277 ntp1021 eth3 20 t33399-s9-persist.pcap udp port 123   (background)
tools/tb tool tb277 ntp1021 ntpc 10.38.22.34 --bind 10.38.22.33 --count 4 --gap 2 --timeout 3

  #1 sent 2026-10-21 03:38:56.817055Z  reply 48 B from 10.38.22.34:123
      LI=0(no-warning) VN=4 mode=4(server) stratum=10 poll=4 precision=-25
      refid=127.127.1.1   origin_echo=yes
      offset=-14.835217 s  delay=0.001627 s
SUMMARY: sent=4 replies=3 origin_echo=3 no_reply=1
         reply stratum(s)=10 mode(s)=4
```
`ntpwire`: `requests=4 replies=3 paired=3 unpaired=0 unanswered=1` -- the one unanswered request
is the step-7 rate limiter, not a persistence failure (a 10-packet follow-up run the same minute
answered 9 of 10 at the same stratum).

**The saved `ntp master 10` survived the cold boot and the DUT was serving at stratum 10, on the
wire, before anyone logged in.** That is reboot persistence measured as behaviour, which is what
the dispatch asked for.

Two by-products worth recording:
- the DUT's **clock survived the reboot** (it has a working RTC) and came back **14.835 s**
  behind the box, against 14.269 s before -- about **0.57 s of drift across one reboot**, with
  no NTP client discipline. T33400 uses this.
- `show reboot history` gained exactly one new row, `2026-10-21 03:37:28 Expected CLI(user
  request)` -- mine, and matched.

**STEP 8 PASS.**

### STEP 9 -- Removal: the service stops, and the counters go dark again

```
awplus(config)#no ntp rate-limit
awplus(config)#no ntp master
awplus#show running-config | include ntp
   (nothing)
awplus#show ntp status
   (completely empty)
awplus#show ntp counters
   (completely empty)
```

On the wire, immediately after:
```
tools/tb tool tb277 ntp1021 ntpc 10.38.22.34 --bind 10.38.22.33 --count 4 --gap 1 --timeout 3
SUMMARY: sent=4 replies=0 origin_echo=0 no_reply=4          (exit 2)
ntpwire: by mode: client(3)=2 ; pairing: requests=2 replies=0 unanswered=2
```
The DUT is silent on UDP/123 again, and `show ntp status` / `show ntp counters` are empty again
-- closing the loop on Finding 1 from the other direction.

**STEP 9 PASS.**

