## Findings for `platforms/x960.md`

1. 🔴 **`show ntp status` and `show ntp counters` print COMPLETELY EMPTY output when NTP is not
   configured** -- no error, no "not configured" message, no header. Measured in both
   directions: empty before `ntp master`, populated immediately after, empty again immediately
   after `no ntp master`. On a 115200 console this is indistinguishable from a hung command, and
   it is the single most likely thing to make a reader think the feature is broken.
2. 🔴🔥 **NTP server rate limiting is ON BY DEFAULT and invisible in the configuration** --
   `burst 8`, `interval 8 s`, `leak 4` (the defaults are only in the `ntp rate-limit ?` help
   text; nothing appears in `show running-config`). A client sending a 20-packet burst 0.5 s
   apart gets **11 answers**. Raising `burst` to 64 gives **20/20** on the identical burst. Any
   NTP measurement on this platform that sends faster than one packet per 8 s will see losses
   that are the product working as designed, and a tester who does not know this will report a
   bench or server fault.
3. 🔥 **`ntp rate-limit` holds exactly ONE keyword.** Setting a second **silently discards the
   first** -- the earlier line simply disappears from `show running-config` with no message and
   no `% ` line, and the device's own log shows the discarded command was accepted. Measured
   three times: `interval 1` then `burst 64` left only `burst 64`; then `leak 2` left only
   `leak 2`; then `interval 1` + `burst 64` left only `burst 64`. The wire agrees with
   running-config each time (16/20 under `leak 2` alone, 20/20 under `burst 64` alone), so it is
   a genuine single-slot configuration, not a display defect. **Same family as group B's
   "a numbered hardware ACL holds exactly ONE rule, silently".**
4. ⚠ **`ntp restrict <addr> deny serve` is rejected; the working order is `<addr> serve deny`.**
   `ntp restrict <addr> ?` offers `allow | deny | query | serve` as if they were siblings, but
   `deny`/`allow` are terminal while `serve`/`query` take `allow|deny` after them. The rejection
   is a plain `% Invalid input detected at '^' marker.`
5. ⚠ **`no ntp restrict` takes the ADDRESS ONLY.** `no ntp restrict 10.38.22.33 serve deny` --
   which is `no ` plus the exact line `show running-config` prints -- is rejected
   (`% Unrecognized command` when probed with `?`). The negation is `no ntp restrict
   <addr>`. **Copying a running-config line and prefixing `no` does not work for this command**,
   which is the normal way a tester removes configuration.
6. ⚠ **`no ntp rate-limit` takes NO keyword**: `no ntp rate-limit burst` is rejected,
   `no ntp rate-limit` succeeds. The same asymmetry as finding 5, in a second command.
7. ⚠ **`ntp rate-limit interval 1 burst 64 leak 1` is rejected**: the keywords cannot be
   combined on one line, they must be separate commands -- and see finding 3 for what then
   happens to them. `leak` is `<2-16>` (so `leak 1` is out of range although the help's default
   text says "allow every 4th request"), `interval` is `<1-4096>` and `burst` is `<1-255>`;
   `interval` and `leak` are both **rounded to a power of 2**.
8. ⚠ **`show ntp counters` counts the CLIENT role only** -- `Client Sent`, `Client Valid
   Received`, `Client Received`. After this DUT had served more than sixty client requests as a
   server, all three counters still read **0**. There is no server-side packet counter on this
   platform, which is precisely why the verdict had to be graded on a capture.
9. **The served refid is `127.127.1.1` (`0x7F7F0101`)**, the classic local-clock reference, and
   `show ntp status` prints it as `Reference ID : 7F7F0101 ()` with an **empty bracket** where a
   name would go.
10. ⚠ **`show ntp status` reports `Precision : 0 (1.000000000 seconds)` while the served packet
    carries `precision = -25`** (about 30 ns). The CLI's precision field and the wire's
    precision field disagree by 25 binary orders of magnitude. Worth a developer's eye; it
    changes nothing for a client, which reads the packet.
11. **`ntp master <n>` is a single-line configuration**: `ntp master 10` replaces `ntp master 5`
    rather than adding to it.
12. **An x960-56YSQ cold-boots to its login prompt in 60 s** (`reload` 03:37:42 -> `login:`
    03:38:43) -- a third measurement agreeing with group C's two. `ckreload.py`'s 720 s default
    is wildly conservative for this platform.
13. **The DUT's clock survives a reboot** (there is a working RTC) and drifted about **0.57 s**
    across one 60 s reboot with no NTP discipline.

